from __future__ import annotations

from thinking_core import render_long, render_note


def investable_portfolio() -> str:
    body = '''<p>Generating AI use cases is rarely the difficult part.</p><p>Workshops produce ideas quickly. Business functions identify opportunities. Soon there are dozens of potential assistants, automations, models and agents competing for attention.</p><p>The leadership problem starts after that.</p><blockquote>Which opportunities deserve investment now, which assumptions should be tested cheaply, and which ideas should not consume capacity at all?</blockquote><p>That is when a use-case list becomes a portfolio.</p><h2>Scoring is useful. It is not the decision.</h2><p>I have seen teams move quickly from ideation into scoring models: business value, feasibility, strategic fit, risk, data readiness.</p><p>The structure helps, but the numbers can create false confidence.</p><p>An idea rated 8/10 for value may still be based mostly on assumptions. Another rated 6/10 may have a committed business owner, accessible data and evidence that a workflow can actually change.</p><p>Those are not equivalent investment propositions.</p><p>I prefer to ask a different question:</p><blockquote>What is the cheapest evidence that could materially change our decision?</blockquote><p>Sometimes it is data availability. Sometimes it is user behaviour. Sometimes the technical solution is straightforward and the real uncertainty is whether the economics still make sense after integration, monitoring, support and process change.</p><p>The purpose of early portfolio management is not to predict everything correctly. It is to reduce the right uncertainty before committing more resources.</p><h2>The evidence should change as the investment grows</h2><p>I do not expect a mature business case from an early idea. I do expect the burden of evidence to increase with the commitment.</p><p><strong>At discovery:</strong> I want to know whether the problem matters, whether there is a credible owner and whether AI is genuinely relevant.</p><p><strong>Before a pilot:</strong> I want a clear assumption to test and a definition of what we need to learn.</p><p><strong>Before production:</strong> the conversation changes: value mechanism, data ownership, controls, workflow change and adoption need to become credible.</p><p><strong>Before scale:</strong> I want evidence that the result is repeatable, economically defensible and operable without exceptional support.</p><p>That progression matters because the decision is not simply whether an idea is “good.”</p><p>The decision is whether it deserves <strong>the next unit of investment</strong>.</p><h2>A portfolio must be able to say no</h2><p>This is where many AI portfolios become weak.</p><p>If every initiative remains active because it has a sponsor, showed technical promise or already consumed effort, capacity gets diluted across too many plausible ideas.</p><p>I want four legitimate decisions in a portfolio conversation:</p><p><strong>Invest</strong> — the evidence supports the next commitment.</p><p><strong>Test</strong> — there is one material uncertainty worth resolving before investing further.</p><p><strong>Hold</strong> — the idea may be credible, but timing, capacity or dependencies make it the wrong priority now.</p><p><strong>Stop</strong> — the evidence no longer justifies attention.</p><p>Stopping work is not a failure of the portfolio.</p><p>A portfolio that never stops anything is usually not prioritizing. It is accumulating.</p><h2>The hardest question is rarely “Can we build it?”</h2><p>Technical feasibility matters, but enterprise value often breaks elsewhere: unclear ownership, weak adoption, process friction, costs that were underestimated or benefits that cannot actually be captured.</p><p>A technically successful AI solution can still be a poor investment.</p><p>That is why the question I want leaders asking is not only:</p><p><strong>Can we build this?</strong></p><p>It is:</p><blockquote>What must become true for this to deserve more investment than the alternatives?</blockquote><p>That is the discipline behind an investable AI portfolio.</p><div class="rule"><strong>The quality of an AI portfolio is visible not in how many ideas it contains, but in where the organization chooses to concentrate — and what it is prepared to stop.</strong></div>'''
    aside = '''<span>Portfolio discipline</span><strong>Four decisions should always be legitimate.</strong><div class="article-questions"><div><strong>Invest</strong> — evidence supports the next commitment.</div><div><strong>Test</strong> — resolve one material uncertainty cheaply.</div><div><strong>Hold</strong> — credible idea, wrong timing or capacity.</div><div><strong>Stop</strong> — evidence no longer supports attention.</div></div><p>The point is not a better scoring spreadsheet. It is a clearer investment conversation.</p>'''
    return render_long("investable_portfolio", body, aside)


def adoption_metric() -> str:
    return render_note(
        "adoption_metric",
        "Usage is a signal. Adoption is a change in the work.",
        "I am cautious when AI adoption is reported mainly through licenses, logins or monthly active users. Those numbers are useful for reach, but they can look healthy while the underlying workflow remains exactly the same. For me, stronger evidence is closer to the work: did a decision become faster or better, did a step disappear, did people stop using the old workaround, did cycle time or rework change, and does the behavior persist after the launch campaign? I still track usage. I just would not call it adoption on its own.",
    )
